// AgentGuard -- the Cedar schema for AgentSpec's guard decisions (plan.md S1.5).
//
// This is the contract between the two halves of the architecture:
//
//   Python side   sensors run the AgentSpec predicates over user_input,
//                 tool_input and intermediate_steps. Impure, arbitrary code.
//   Cedar side    a decision over the *result* of that work. Pure and total --
//                 no regex, no AST walking, no I/O.
//
// The schema is what makes the second half checkable. Every policy is validated
// against it before the agent takes a single step, so the failure modes recorded
// in tests/test_fail_open.py (a rule that loads fine and then never fires, or
// crashes mid-run) become load-time errors instead. See docs/spikes.md S1.3 for
// what the validator actually catches.
//
// Deliberately smaller than the thesis design (§C.2). Deferred, with the step
// that adds it:
//
//   Session entity + Agent.session   S4.2   path sensitivity (taints, approvals)
//   Agent.autonomy, Agent.model      S2.2   escalation policies need them
//   Tool.name                        S2.2   the uid carries it for now
//   context.targets / risk / step    S2.2   generated from the sensor registry
//   action finish                    S2.7   `trigger finish` rules
//
// `flags: Set<String>` is also provisional and known to be the weaker of the two
// options: S1.3 showed the validator cannot catch a misspelled *set member*, so a
// typo'd flag name yields a policy that silently never fires. S2.2 replaces it
// with a record of named Bools generated from the predicate registry, which does
// catch it. It stays a set here because S1.5 asks for the smallest schema that
// works, and swapping it is the point of the S2.2 comparison.

namespace AgentGuard {

  // The agent being guarded. One per run.
  entity Agent = {
    framework: String,          // "langchain" -- the only binding wired up so far
  };

  // The tool it is proposing to invoke. The uid is AgentSpec's tool name, so
  // `trigger python_repl` compiles to resource == Tool::"python_repl".
  entity Tool = {
    kind: String,               // "code_exec" | "shell" | "http" | "actuator" | "read_only"
    reversible: Bool,           // can the effect be undone?
  };

  // One proposed tool call. The AgentSpec `check` clause lands in context.flags:
  // each predicate that fired contributes its name.
  action invoke appliesTo {
    principal: [Agent],
    resource:  [Tool],
    context: {
      flags: Set<String>,
    }
  };
}
